theory.intro:
Circle formulas such as $A = \pi r^{2}$ use the **radius**. When a question gives the **diameter** instead, halve it first, before any other calculation.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw[thick] (O) circle (1.8);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (180:1.8) -- (0:1.8) node[midway, below, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$d$};
\draw (O) -- (60:1.8) node[midway, above left, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
\draw[thick] (0,0) circle (1.5);
\fill (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (180:1.5)--(0:1.5) node[midway,below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$10\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
State the radius of this circle, in centimetres.

foundation.solution_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
\draw[thick] (0,0) circle (1.5);
\fill (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (180:1.5)--(0:1.5);
\node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.75,0) {$5\text{ cm}$};
\node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (0.75,0) {$5\text{ cm}$};
\node[above] at (0,0.18) {$10\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$r = \frac{d}{2}$
$= \frac{10}{2}$
$= 5\text{ cm}$

